beaucoup de recherche pour rien.
on a utilise ghidra
on test objdump -x level10

bref, level10 commence par utiliser access() pour verifier les droits du user.
=> man access 
NOTES
       Warning: Using access() to check if a user is authorized to, for example, open a file before actually doing so
       using open(2) creates a security hole, because the user might exploit the short time interval between checking
       and  opening  the file to manipulate it.  For this reason, the use of this system call should be avoided.  (In
       the example just described, a safer alternative would be to temporarily switch the process's effective user ID
       to the real ID and then call open(2).)

on a trouve sur le web un script qui utilise cet exploit, identifie as TOCOU.
https://resources.infosecinstitute.com/topic/race-condition-toctou-vulnerability-lab/

on creer un lien symbolique vers le token
on bash le script ./exploit.sh

You don't have access to passwdlocal
You don't have access to passwdlocal
You don't have access to passwdlocal
You don't have access to passwdlocal
Connecting to 127.0.0.1:6969 .. Connected!
Sending file .. wrote file!
You don't have access to passwdlocal
You don't have access to passwdlocal
You don't have access to passwdlocal


